#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:1
msgid "Invariants before algorithms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:4
msgid "A useful invariant says what stays true while the algorithm runs. It also says what the caller may rely on when the algorithm stops. Write it before writing the loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:8
msgid "Preorder subtree intervals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:11
msgid "Assume a tree is emitted in preorder. Every node is emitted before its children. Assume each subtree is contiguous. Let :math:`e_i` be the first index after node :math:`i` and all its descendants."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:15
msgid "\\operatorname{subtree}(i) = [i,e_i), \\qquad i < e_i \\le N."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:20
msgid "**Claim.** A traversal can skip the subtree by advancing to :math:`e_i`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:22
msgid "**Proof.** Contiguity puts every descendant before :math:`e_i`. The exclusive-end definition puts every later node at or after :math:`e_i`. Advancing there therefore skips exactly the subtree. The validator checks the assumptions before a renderer relies on them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:29
msgid "Document zero contains heading and paragraph subtrees, each with a text leaf. Five preorder nodes occupy nested half-open intervals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:29
msgid "A parent's interval encloses its children's intervals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:50
msgid "A complete scan visits each node once. Its traversal cost is :math:`O(N)`. Skipping a subtree takes one index update. Payload work still costs what it uses. Do not confuse a constant-time skip with constant-time rendering."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:54
msgid "Measure before emit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:57
msgid "The renderer first measures the byte count :math:`m`. Let the caller's output capacity be :math:`c`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:60
msgid "m \\le c \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\text{emission may begin}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:64
msgid "The measured and emitted traversals use the same rules. A successful emission must write exactly :math:`m` bytes. A failure returns no valid partial artifact. The host owns publication, so incomplete bytes cannot replace a previous output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:69
msgid "Cache validity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:72
msgid "A cache key must cover the data that can change a result. Sources alone are insufficient. Includes, templates, configuration, reference lookups, resources, and adapter versions can affect the output. Reuse is allowed only when the recorded dependencies still agree. A missing output invalidates reuse even when its key matches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/algorithms.rst:78
msgid "Tests install failing allocators at the core boundary and compare emitted content. They also audit imports. Counting allocations without checking the result could miss a refusal that silently discarded part of the document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:1
msgid "The Guidedoc engine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:4
msgid "Guidedoc is the conversion core. It borrows storage from its caller. It reads a source, builds a semantic tree, runs passes, measures the output, and emits bytes. It does not open files or publish artifacts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:10
msgid "The CLI calls the project host, which supplies fixed storage to the conversion core."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:10
msgid "Effects belong to the host; document semantics belong to Guidedoc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:29
msgid "Conversion data flow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:32
msgid "A document passes through distinct phases within the Guidedoc conversion core. Each stage borrows memory from the caller's pre-allocated workspace buffers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:37
msgid "Borrowed source becomes a flat tree. Passes transform it. A measurement determines the output capacity before emission."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:37
msgid "Conversion data flow within the Guidedoc core."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:57
msgid "The boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:60
msgid "Core conversion performs no allocation. The caller supplies node columns, text storage, diagnostics, and output capacity. A host may allocate while preparing those buffers. A third-party compiler has its own allocation rules. The guarantee names the conversion boundary, not the whole process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:66
msgid "The tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:69
msgid "Nodes have typed IDs. Hot columns hold the kind and subtree range. Cold tables hold payloads needed by particular node kinds. A flat preorder representation lets a traversal skip a whole subtree without following a chain of heap pointers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:74
msgid "For node :math:`i`, let :math:`e_i` be its exclusive subtree end. The subtree occupies :math:`[i,e_i)`. A leaf has :math:`e_i=i+1`. A parent closes after its descendants. See :doc:`algorithms` for the interval proof."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:79
msgid "The API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:82
msgid "The core API is explicit: initialize storage, convert, inspect the result, reset. Registries are values containing reader, pass, and renderer descriptors. There is no runtime plugin discovery or global registration side effect. The small set of verbs is meant to remain easy to use from another Odin program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/engine.rst:87
msgid "Ownership is the key rule. Views into a workspace expire on reset or reuse. A result held by the host must own any text that outlives the session. Use :doc:`../api/core` for exact signatures. It is generated from the same Odin source as the separate ``docs/api`` project. The manual's PDF includes that reference in the same book, with internal links. The design record is GDS 0003 in ``docs/gds``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/index.rst:1
msgid "Internals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/index.rst:4
msgid "These chapters explain the reasoning behind the implementation. They describe current contracts. GDS records retain decisions and review states in a separate project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:1
msgid "Memory and ownership"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:4
msgid "A budget is an accounting contract. It has meaning only when the lifetime of a charge matches the lifetime of the storage it pays for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:9
msgid "A managed budget accounts for arenas and conversion buffers, backed by RAM or memory-mapped disk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:9
msgid "The host reserves working storage before allocating it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:27
msgid "Managed host storage shares a process with native libraries. Installed plugins and the development Typst backend use child processes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:27
msgid "Allocation accounting and process isolation are different boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:43
msgid "A charge follows storage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:46
msgid "Reserve bytes before allocating. Release the charge after freeing the storage. When replacing a buffer, the old and new buffers can coexist. Charge the full new allocation during that overlap. Charging only the increase would understate the peak."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:51
msgid ""
"\\text{replacement peak} = \\text{other live storage}\n"
"  + \\text{old buffer} + \\text{new buffer}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:56
msgid "Session-owned input is reclaimed by the session. Caller-owned input remains the caller's responsibility. The names ``read_owned`` and ``read_kept`` make that distinction explicit. A shorter read does not shrink the allocation already made for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:61
msgid "Known zero is not unknown"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:64
msgid "A memory probe can know that no headroom remains. It can also fail to discover headroom. Those are different states. The policy carries a separate knowledge flag rather than using zero for both. On Linux, readable cgroup limits apply through the visible ancestor hierarchy. A container's host-wide free-memory number alone can be misleading."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:70
msgid "Reports outlive workspaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:73
msgid "A report shown after a session ends must own its title, source, and repair text. It must not borrow a buffer that the session has freed. Source spans use byte offsets internally. User-facing columns count Unicode scalars. Terminal markers account for display width separately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:78
msgid "The remaining boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/memory.rst:81
msgid "Guidedog meters its managed working storage. The Typst compiler and native dependencies manage their own allocations. Graphviz and tree-sitter run native code in the host process. The release's embedded Typst compiler does too; the development backend can use a subprocess. An installed plugin runs in a separate process. These are different isolation boundaries, even though their native allocations fall outside the host ledger. Large PDF books can consume much more memory than the host budget. The remote CPython validation measured about 14.5 GiB peak RSS including Typst. Treat that measurement as workload evidence, not a universal upper bound. See :doc:`../errors` for the user's choices when a managed budget is reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:1
msgid "Publish one complete generation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:4
msgid "A reader should never see half an updated site. This is a publication property. It is separate from parsing and rendering."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:7
msgid "The invariant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:10
msgid "Let :math:`P` be the published output tree. During preparation, :math:`P` remains unchanged. The next tree :math:`Q` is complete before the commit is decided. The commit journal names the output and the records that belong with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:14
msgid "**Preparation can fail.** The host abandons the unfinished generation. **A decided commit can be interrupted.** The next build finishes or restores it from the journal. It does not guess from a staging directory's name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:20
msgid "Prepare and flush, save journal, switch output, replace records, then remove journal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:20
msgid "The durable journal separates preparation from a decided commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:38
msgid "Why the cache is outside the output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:41
msgid "A directory exchange moves everything inside the publication directory. A journal placed inside it would move during the very operation it describes. Its location would cease to be stable when recovery needed it most. Guidedog keeps records in a stable directory outside the publication tree. A custom output uses a sibling cache for this reason."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:47
msgid "Durability and visibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:50
msgid "Flushing file contents is not the same as flushing directory names. The algorithm accounts for both before removing the journal. Platforms differ in how directories are exchanged. The recovery protocol is designed around those differences. See ``lib/project/commit.odin`` and ``lib/project/publish.odin`` for the implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:56
msgid "This protects the project publication. It is not a distributed transaction with an external hosting provider. Copying a successful build to a server is another operation, owned by the deployment tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../internals/publication.rst:60
msgid "On filesystems with directory exchange, the output path switches in one operation. The fallback uses two renames and can briefly leave that path absent. It still never exposes a partly assembled tree. Journal recovery finishes or restores an interrupted switch. A client making several requests can span two generations; the build does not promise a snapshot for an entire browsing session."
msgstr ""
